# Peka — Co-op 2D Platform Puzzler

> Game Design Document (living draft). Filled out as we ideate.
>
> **Decided:** symmetric players · local couch · eerie tone · lightless caverns · mirrored-gravity core (flip-at-will, mid-air redirect · shared tunnel · fall = death) · tight movement · lethal surface-bound threats · darkness = atmosphere

---

## 1. Description

A **2D co-op platform game** built around **cooperative puzzles**. Two players
travel together through **one long level** in a system of **lightless caverns**.
Each player can **flip their own gravity at will** — even mid-air, redirecting a
fall — sticking to the floor or ceiling of a **shared tunnel** with a lethal gap
between. The level is **impossible to complete solo**: clearing it requires two
bodies oriented and positioned in ways one player can never achieve alone.

Tone is **mysterious and eerie** — sparse, atmospheric, told through the
environment rather than text (Limbo / Inside lineage). Two small, vulnerable
figures; a large, unexplained dark.

**Core pillars**
- **Forced cooperation** — no section clears without two players.
- **Flip-at-will gravity** — the signature verb; floor/ceiling is fluid.
- **Puzzle-first platforming** — movement is the verb; the brain-teaser is the challenge.
- **One handcrafted journey** — a single, long, escalating level.
- **Wordless atmosphere** — eerie mood, environmental storytelling, minimal UI.

---

## 2. Cooperation Model — ✅ Symmetric + Mirrored Gravity

Identical characters. Either **flips their own gravity anytime, including
mid-air** (redirecting a fall), landing on floor or ceiling of one continuous
**shared tunnel**. Falling into the central gap with no surface to catch you, or
onto a hazard, = **death → respawn together at last checkpoint**.

### ⚠️ Central design tension — forcing cooperation
Free flipping means a careless puzzle is **soloable**. Co-op is enforced by
designing around what **one body cannot do**:
- **Simultaneity** — two inputs held *at once* on opposite surfaces; one body, one place.
- **Bodies as platforms** — be your partner's landing / ledge across the lethal gap.
- **Asymmetric reach** — your input clears the *other's* hazard or builds their crossing.
This discipline is the heart of level design — solved for in every puzzle.

### Cooperative verbs — _working set_
- **Hold-two-at-once** — simultaneous opposite-surface plates/switches.
- **Be the landing / bridge** — partner is the platform across the gap.
- **Build the crossing** — one holds a ledge/mechanism out so the other crosses.
- **Clear the path** — your input removes the other's hazard / opens their gate.
- **Coordinated flip** — flip together to move a shared counterweight.

---

## 3. Mechanics — ✅ locked

### Movement — tight & minimal
**Run · single jump · flip gravity.** That's the kit. The flip (incl. mid-air
redirect) is the depth. No double-jump / wall-cling. Instantly learnable; puzzles
stay about coordination, not execution.

### Visibility — atmosphere only
Ambient bioluminescence; you can always see. Dark is **mood**, not a mechanic.
Keeps gravity the single star system.

### Threat — shadow creatures, lethal on touch
**Surface-bound**, **instant death on contact** (Limbo dread). Each player avoids
the creatures on their own surface; they add **timing pressure**, not the core
co-op (puzzles do that). Often a partner's input controls/lures the other's threat.

### Failure & respawn — instant death, shared checkpoints
Any death (gap or creature) respawns **both** players at the last checkpoint.

### Input
Local couch → **two gamepads**, each with run / jump / **flip**.

